# Verification toolbox

<!-- Copy to ~/.agents/verification-toolbox.md and fill in. This file is
untracked on purpose: it names the tools installed on your machines and the
project precedents that earned each one a place. Skills and project guides
read the filled copy when planning gates and test harnesses. -->

The reusable tools and packages for verification tasks, one section per
capability. For each entry record the tool, the language or surface it
covers, and the precedent: which project used it and what it caught. Mark
anything unconfirmed *(verify)* and version-check it at adoption time.

## External conformance harness

How real binaries are driven from outside: the default harness language and
runner, pty tooling for TUIs, and how daemons are driven (prefer the real
socket over an in-process shortcut).

- <harness default, e.g. Python stdlib with unittest>
- <pty tooling for TUI binaries>

## Property-based testing

One entry per language lane: the PBT library, and where properties live when
a lane has no mature one.

- <language: library, state-machine support, precedent>

## Fuzzing

Native fuzzers per lane, corpus and seed conventions, and which CI runs a
fuzz smoke.

- <language: fuzzer, corpus location, named regression seeds>

## Golden / snapshot testing

The fixture pattern (framework-free byte-compare travels best across
languages) and any in-language snapshot libraries accepted.

- <pattern, fixture location, promotion rule>

## Differential and oracle testing

Dual implementations kept as oracles, pinned reference implementations, and
replay corpora.

- <oracle, the surface it guards>

## Sanitizers and memory discipline

Thread, address, and UB sanitizers per lane; allocator-failure and leak
checks; RSS regression tools.

- <sanitizer or check: build flag and where it runs>

## Performance budgets

Wall-time, RSS, allocation, and compile-time budget tools, each with where
its threshold lives.

- <tool: the budget it enforces>

## Lint and structure gates

Format checks, static analysis, vulnerability scans, and repo-local
structural lints, all in the gate script from day one.

- <check: the gate it runs in>

## CI

Where the gate battery runs on push. Keep the gate script the single source
of truth so local verification and CI can never disagree.

## Diagnostic CLIs

Installed diagnostic tools worth reaching for before writing a one-off
script, with their domains.

- <tool: domain>

Note: this is a living inventory, neither all-inclusive nor restrictive. When
research or a new lane surfaces a better tool for a capability, propose it,
and ask the owner before installing anything system-wide.
